                <pb facs="0571" n="489" sameAs="frus:frus1911/pg489"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/1888.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The American Ambassador to the Secretary of
                        State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1911-05-22"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">May 22,
                    1911</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>The treaty of peace was signed at 10 o’clock last night. It may therefore be said
                    that the revolution is ended, though it is yet to be shown whether the more
                    formidable rebel chiefs can be controlled by the Maderists. Two-thirds of the
                    Republic is now in the hands of the revolutionists; the President is to transfer
                    the Government to Provisional President de la Barra on Wednesday or
                    Thursday.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
